# Trivy CVE allow-list for upstream-pending issues that don't apply to
# our threat model. Each entry MUST include:
#   - the CVE ID
#   - which artifact it appears in (manager / tools)
#   - upstream tracking link
#   - threat-model justification (why it doesn't matter for THIS deployment)
#   - planned removal trigger
#
# These ignores are honored by trivy fs / image scans cluster-wide. Keep
# the list short — every entry is a declared accepted risk; review on
# every release.
#
# ==========================================================================
# cosign v2.6.3 stdlib (Go 1.25.7) — keystone-tools image only
# ==========================================================================
#
# cosign v2.6.3 (latest v2.x maintenance release, 2026-04-06 — same day
# as v3.0.6) is built with Go 1.25.7. The four CVEs below were patched
# in Go 1.25.8 (CVE-2026-25679) and Go 1.25.9 (CVE-2026-32280 / 32281
# / 32283). sigstore has not yet published a rebuild on 1.25.9+ on
# either the v2.x or v3.x line; the keystone-manager image is on Go
# 1.26.2 and is unaffected.
#
# WHY v2.6.3 NOT v3.0.6
# ---------------------
# cosign v3 deprecated `--tlog-upload=false` in favor of `--signing-config`
# files, which would require refactoring every signing call site here.
# v2.6.3 is the LTS-line maintenance release shipped alongside v3.0.6 with
# the same dependency-CVE backports but unchanged v2 flag syntax. When
# sigstore eventually drops v2.x support we'll refactor to v3 + signing-
# config in one go.
#
# THREAT MODEL FOR THIS DEPLOYMENT
# --------------------------------
# cosign in keystone-tools is invoked exclusively by the soak CronJob
# (<gitops-repo>/keystone/resources/soak/cronjob.yaml).
# The Pod:
#   - exposes no network listeners (it's a one-shot job)
#   - parses image references from `kubectl get` jsonpath output
#     (operator-controlled, in-cluster admin SA — not attacker-supplied)
#   - performs only `cosign verify` against Harbor (in-cluster TLS via
#     Linkerd mTLS to harbor.monitoring.svc, not a public endpoint)
#   - holds no secrets beyond the soak ServiceAccount token
#
# All four CVEs require attacker-controlled untrusted input on a TLS
# server side or attacker-controlled URL in net/url.Parse. None of those
# code paths exist in our cosign-as-soak-CLI usage. Risk acceptance is
# bounded by:
#   - 7-day weekly soak cadence (CronJob window)
#   - revocable RBAC (kubectl delete clusterrolebinding keystone-soak)
#   - Loki log audit trail (every soak run is observable)
#
# REMOVAL TRIGGER
# ---------------
# Remove all four lines when sigstore publishes a cosign release built
# with Go 1.25.9+ (likely v3.0.7 or v3.1.0). Track:
#   https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/releases
#
# Verify post-removal with:
#   trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed --exit-code 1 \
#     ghcr.io/dogukanturhal/keystone-tools:v0.1.x
CVE-2026-25679
CVE-2026-32280
CVE-2026-32281
CVE-2026-32283

# ==========================================================================
# cosign v2.6.3 transitive deps — keystone-tools image only
# ==========================================================================
#
# 5 dep CVEs that cosign v2.6.3 + v3.0.6 (both released 2026-04-06) have
# not yet bumped. Tracked upstream:
#   https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3Aupgrade
#
# Per-CVE attack surface analysis for OUR usage of cosign — exclusively
# as a one-shot CronJob CLI in keystone-tools (soak Pod). The Pod:
#   - has no network listener
#   - talks to Harbor via HTTP(S) — HTTPS to upstream Harbor, NOT gRPC
#   - sets --tlog-upload=false (no Rekor / public sigstore calls)
#   - reads image refs from `kubectl get` jsonpath (operator-controlled)
#
# CVE-2025-15558  docker/cli v29.0.3 -> 29.2.0
#                 Privilege escalation in Docker CLI for WINDOWS only.
#                 Our containers are Linux. Zero impact.
#
# CVE-2026-34986  go-jose/v4 v4.1.3 -> 4.1.4
#                 JWS signature verification edge case affecting
#                 servers that accept attacker-supplied JWS. cosign in
#                 our usage VERIFIES signatures it produced itself,
#                 with publicKeys we control. No untrusted JWS path.
#
# CVE-2026-24051  otel/sdk v1.38.0 -> 1.40.0
#                 Arbitrary log file read via OTEL collector config
#                 manipulation. No OTEL collector deployed in our soak
#                 path. cosign's otel SDK is unused at runtime.
#
# CVE-2026-39883  otel/sdk v1.38.0 -> 1.43.0
#                 BSD `kenv(2)` command not using safe path. Linux-only
#                 deployment. Zero impact.
#
# CVE-2026-33186  grpc v1.77.0 -> 1.79.3 (CRITICAL)
#                 gRPC HTTP/2 server resource exhaustion. cosign uses
#                 gRPC as a CLIENT to TUF/Rekor. With --tlog-upload=
#                 false no Rekor traffic flows. OCI/Harbor traffic is
#                 plain HTTPS, not gRPC. No gRPC server exposed.
#
# REMOVAL TRIGGER
# ---------------
# Each of these is fixed upstream. cosign needs to bump the dep and
# re-release. Track sigstore/cosign Dependabot PRs. Remove the
# matching ignore line as each new cosign release ships the fix.
CVE-2025-15558
CVE-2026-34986
CVE-2026-24051
CVE-2026-39883
CVE-2026-33186

# ==========================================================================
# 2026-05-12 Go stdlib batch — cosign + kubectl in keystone-tools
# ==========================================================================
#
# Five HIGH stdlib CVEs published 2026-05-12 affect every upstream Go
# binary built before that date:
#   cosign v3.0.6 (Go 1.25.7) — sigstore/cosign latest, no rebuild
#   kubectl v1.34.x/1.35.x/1.36.x (Go 1.25.9 / 1.26.2) — k8s.io latest,
#                                                       no rebuild
# Fixed in Go 1.25.10 or 1.26.3. The keystone-manager image is already
# rebuilt on Go 1.26.3 (Dockerfile bump) and is clean; these ignores
# are scoped functionally to the keystone-tools image.
#
# THREAT MODEL FOR THIS DEPLOYMENT
# --------------------------------
# Same as the cosign v2.6.3 stdlib section above:
#   - cosign and kubectl in keystone-tools are invoked exclusively by the
#     soak CronJob, a one-shot in-cluster admin Pod with no network
#     listener and operator-controlled inputs only.
#   - kubectl talks to the in-cluster API server via Linkerd mTLS, with
#     RBAC scoped to the soak SA's read permissions.
#   - cosign uses --tlog-upload=false (no Rekor / public sigstore).
#   - The Pod has no attacker surface and parses no untrusted data.
# All five CVEs require attacker-controlled inputs (TLS server, HTTP
# parser, x509 chain) on a code path our usage does not enter.
#
# REMOVAL TRIGGER
# ---------------
# Remove all five lines when both upstream rebuilds land:
#   - cosign on Go 1.25.10+ (likely v3.0.7 or v3.1.0)
#   - kubectl on Go 1.25.10 / 1.26.3 (next k8s patch wave)
# Track:
#   https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/releases
#   https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/releases
#
# These also gate sign-image via vuln-scan-tools-image (the CI configuration
# sign-image.needs). Without them, every master pipeline produces an
# unsigned image that Kyverno verify-keystone-image-signatures denies.
CVE-2026-33811
CVE-2026-33814
CVE-2026-39820
CVE-2026-39836
CVE-2026-42499

